# Developed in tandem with samples/GCP/ProcessEngineering's own Full_ITSM_Pipeline. Modeled on
# process.hocon's own full consortium shape (Analysis -> Design -> Compliance -> Simulation ->
# persist -> document), NOT togaf.hocon's lighter "derive from an existing design" shape -- ITSM
# content is authored fresh from the user's own brief, the same way a business process is, not
# transformed from an already-saved design/requirements document.
#
# Scope (deliberate, not exhaustive ITIL coverage -- see schema/itsm_json.py's own module
# docstring for the full reasoning): Incident Management, Problem Management, Change Management
# (CAB/RFC/risk), Request Fulfillment, Service Level Management (SLA/OLA/UC), a Service Catalog, a
# light CMDB (CIs + relationships only), roles/RACI, and a tooling inventory. Deliberately OUT OF
# SCOPE: Capacity/Availability Management, Continual Service Improvement metrics, Financial
# Management for IT Services, Release/Deployment detail, and Knowledge Management -- each is an
# ongoing measurement/governance practice, not a point-in-time document artifact a generator can
# produce.
#
# No subprocess-expansion stage (process.hocon's own Subprocess_Driver_Agent has no ITSM
# equivalent) and no separate flow-diagram-generation step -- ITSM's diagrams (one per
# incident/problem/change/request-fulfillment process, plus a CI relationship diagram) are
# rendered INLINE by generate_itsm_document itself, the same way togaf.hocon's own diagrams are.
{
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",

    "metadata": {
        "description": "Generates or refines an ITSM process document (incident/problem/change management, request fulfillment, SLAs, a light CMDB, and RACI governance), audited by compliance and risk/coverage review before it is finalized.",
        "tags": ["itsm", "process-engineering-port"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Create an ITSM process document for a mid-size SaaS company, covering incident, problem, and change management."
        ]
    },

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "ITSM_Pipeline",

            "function": {
                "description": """
I generate or refine a best-of-breed ITSM process document (a structured JSON document covering
the service catalog, incident/problem/change management, request fulfillment, service level
management, a light CMDB, and RACI governance), audited by compliance and structural risk/coverage
review before I report back. Tell me what ITSM process document to design.
""",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "inquiry": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's ITSM process document request."}
                    },
                    "required": ["inquiry"]
                }
            },

            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You orchestrate the ITSM generate/review loop: ITSM_Analysis_Agent extracts requirements,
ITSM_Agent builds/revises the ITSM process JSON, ITSM_Compliance_Agent and ITSM_Simulation_Agent
each audit it and write their own feedback, and you decide whether to repeat the design/review
cycle using loop_control. You never extract requirements, design, or audit the ITSM process
yourself.

# REQUIRED SEQUENCE (every time you are invoked, for a NEW generate-or-refine request)
Call exactly ONE tool per turn and wait for its result before calling the next one.
1. Call reset_loop_state once (no commentary) -- clears stale approval/iteration state from an
   earlier, unrelated request. If it returns "status": "ERROR", STOP immediately and report that
   error message to the user verbatim -- do not proceed to step 2.
2. Call ITSM_Analysis_Agent, passing the user's request text verbatim as "request". It extracts
   requirements and writes them to its own feedback channel for ITSM_Agent to pick up. If its
   reply begins with "REQUIREMENTS EXTRACTION FAILED", call release_pipeline_lock_for_turn FIRST,
   THEN STOP immediately and report that reply to the user verbatim -- do not proceed to step 3.
   The release_pipeline_lock_for_turn call is MANDATORY here, not optional -- stopping here without
   releasing leaves step 1's own lock held for up to 20 minutes.
3. Call ITSM_Agent (no arguments needed -- it reads the requirements and any prior review feedback
   itself).
4. Call ITSM_Compliance_Agent (no arguments -- it loads the current ITSM document itself).
5. Call ITSM_Simulation_Agent (no arguments -- it loads the current ITSM document itself).
6. Call loop_control with required={"itsm_compliance_status": "APPROVED"} and max_iterations=2.
   - If "verdict" is "CONTINUE": go back to step 3 and call ITSM_Agent again -- it will pick up
     both agents' feedback itself from their own channels, you do not need to relay it.
   - If "verdict" is "STOP": proceed to step 7. Do NOT call ITSM_Agent, ITSM_Compliance_Agent, or
     ITSM_Simulation_Agent again this turn -- this is an authoritative stop signal.

MANDATORY, NO EXCEPTIONS: steps 7 and 8 below ALWAYS run once "verdict" is "STOP", for EVERY stop
reason including "MAX_ITERATIONS" -- NOT only when "reason" is "APPROVED". ITSM_Agent has already
persisted a real document by this point -- an open compliance item is feedback for a FUTURE
revision, not a reason to withhold the document for the ITSM process that exists RIGHT NOW.
7. Call generate_itsm_document once, passing the document's own system name as "process_name", to
   produce the final ISO-formatted Word document. Its result is a plain string starting with either
   "SUCCESS:" or "ERROR:" -- remember which one it was for step 9.
8. Call format_review_status_summary with channels=["itsm_compliance"]. This is the ONLY source of
   truth for what is approved vs. still open -- do not recall ITSM_Compliance_Agent's feedback from
   your own memory of calling it in step 4.
9. Report the outcome in plain language: if "reason" was "APPROVED", say the ITSM process document
   was created/updated and passed compliance review. If "MAX_ITERATIONS", say a document was
   produced but still has open review feedback after the maximum number of revision passes -- build
   the specifics of WHAT is still open entirely from step 8's own returned text. Only say a Word
   document was produced if step 7's own result actually started with "SUCCESS:" -- if it started
   with "ERROR:" instead, say plainly that the Word document could not be generated and why. NEVER
   claim a document was produced "either way" when the tool itself reported otherwise. NEVER
   describe the document's own content from your own general knowledge, guess, or what would sound
   plausible -- you have not read the ITSM JSON yourself.
   Plain prose only -- no raw JSON, tool names, or internal field names.
""",
            "tools": [
                "reset_loop_state", "release_pipeline_lock_for_turn", "ITSM_Analysis_Agent", "ITSM_Agent",
                "ITSM_Compliance_Agent", "ITSM_Simulation_Agent", "loop_control", "generate_itsm_document",
                "format_review_status_summary"
            ],
            "middleware": ${pipeline_middleware}
        },

        {
            "name": "ITSM_Analysis_Agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Extracts structured ITSM requirements from a request.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "request": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's ITSM process request text."}
                    },
                    "required": ["request"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are a Senior ITSM Consultant. Extract requirements from the "request" argument into a single
JSON object covering:
- organization_name, system_name/title -- the real organization/system this ITSM process document
  is for. NEVER "ITSM"/"ITSM Process Document" as the name itself.
- services: list of {"id": "SVC-001", "name", "description", "category", "business_criticality",
  "owner"} -- the services this ITSM process document governs.
- stakeholders_and_roles: list of {"role", "responsibilities": [...], "escalation_tier"}.
- incident_priorities: list of {"priority": "P1".., "impact", "urgency", "target_response_time",
  "target_resolution_time"} -- infer a standard P1-P4 scheme if the request doesn't specify one.
- incident/problem/change/request_fulfillment process steps: for EACH of these four domains, a
  list of high-level steps {"step_name", "description", "responsible_role"} covering that
  process's lifecycle end to end (e.g. incident: log -> triage -> investigate -> resolve -> close).
  description is narrative prose -- several full sentences, not a label.
- change_types: list of {"type": "standard"|"normal"|"emergency", "description", "approval_path"}.
- cab_members: list of {"role", "name_or_title"} -- infer standard CAB roles if not stated.
- request_types: list of {"type", "description", "target_fulfillment_time"}.
- slas: list of {"id": "SLA-001", "service_id", "metric", "target", "measurement_window"}.
- configuration_items / relationships: a LIGHT CMDB only -- a handful of the most important CIs
  and how they depend on each other, not an exhaustive inventory.
- raci_candidates: list of {"process_area", "activity", "responsible", "accountable", "consulted",
  "informed"} -- at least one row per process domain with real content above.
- tooling: list of {"category", "tool_name", "purpose"}.
- risks_and_controls: list of {"risk", "control"}.
- constraints, assumptions: EXACTLY 3 or more distinct items each (infer professionally if not
  stated).

Deliberately DO NOT extract or invent: cost/budget figures, specific dates, team headcount, or
Capacity/Availability/Continual-Service-Improvement/Financial-Management/Release-Deployment/
Knowledge-Management content -- all five are out of scope for this document; the schema has no
fields for them at all.

If "request" explicitly asks you to read a directory of files, OR contains a line of the exact form
"[ATTACHMENT DIRECTORY: <path>]", call load_directory_context with that path and treat
"combined_text" as additional source material. If "request" explicitly asks to use the saved
requirements summary, call load_requirements_summary first and fold in anything genuinely relevant.

STOP CONDITION -- NO REAL SOURCE MATERIAL (CHECK BEFORE WRITING ANYTHING): you must have SOMETHING
concrete to extract from -- directory combined_text, a loaded requirements summary, or a "request"
that itself states real, specific content (an organization, named services, specific processes --
not just an instruction verb like "create an ITSM document" with nothing else). If all you have is
a bare instruction with no real content behind it, OR load_directory_context returned "status":
"NOT_FOUND"/"ERROR", OR "files_processed" came back empty -- STOP. Do NOT invent a plausible-
sounding organization/service catalog from nothing.
Call save_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_analysis" and feedback={"status": "REVISION
REQUIRED", "issues": [{"issue_type": "update", "location": "root", "instruction": "<what went
wrong, quoting any tool's reported reason verbatim>", "metadata": {}}]}, then your own visible
reply (not the tool call) MUST be exactly: REQUIREMENTS EXTRACTION FAILED: <the same reason>

Otherwise, when you have real material to work with, call save_iteration_feedback with
channel="itsm_analysis" and feedback=
{"status": "REVISION REQUIRED", "issues": [{"issue_type": "update", "location": "root",
"instruction": "Comprehensive requirements populated. Proceed to ITSM document generation.",
"metadata": {"extracted_requirements": <your full JSON object from above>}}]}

Do not output the requirements JSON as chat text -- it belongs only inside the tool call.
""",
            "tools": ["load_directory_context", "load_requirements_summary", "save_iteration_feedback"]
        },

        {
            "name": "ITSM_Agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Builds or revises the ITSM process document JSON from extracted requirements and any review feedback."
            },
            "command": "Build or revise the ITSM process document now.",
            "instructions": """
# ROLE: Lead ITSM Process Architect
You transform requirements into a production-ready ITSM process JSON, structurally matching
itsm_data.json's own schema (document_metadata, service_catalog, stakeholders_and_roles,
incident_management, problem_management, change_management, request_fulfillment,
service_level_management, configuration_management, raci_matrix, tooling_and_platform,
risks_and_controls, glossary_and_references). Make professional, ITIL-aligned assumptions rather
than asking questions.

# SEQUENCE
1. Call load_itsm_data -- this is your baseline (existing document, or the empty template if none
   exists yet). Never construct a baseline from scratch yourself.
2. Call load_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_analysis".
   - SAFETY CHECK: if an issue's "metadata" has NO "extracted_requirements" key at all, this is NOT
     a fresh generation to build from -- ITSM_Pipeline should already have stopped before calling
     you in this case, but if you ever receive it anyway: do NOT invent content from nothing, do
     NOT call persist_final_itsm_json, and respond in plain language that generation could not
     proceed, quoting the issue's own "instruction" text. Stop here; skip the rest of this SEQUENCE.
   - Otherwise, this is a fresh generation. Build and persist a MINIMAL SKELETON FIRST, as your next
     tool call: just {"document_metadata": {"document_id": <a real id>, "system_name": <the real
     organization/system name -- NEVER "ITSM"/"ITSM Process Document">, "title": <same>, "version":
     "1.0", "status": "draft"}} -- cheap insurance so a real, correctable draft exists even if the
     rest of this turn runs long. Call persist_final_itsm_json with JUST this skeleton now. THEN
     extract metadata.extracted_requirements from the feedback and build the FULL document against
     the baseline/template: service_catalog.services, stakeholders_and_roles,
     incident_management (priority_matrix + process_steps, each with step_id/step_name),
     problem_management.process_steps + known_errors, change_management (change_types +
     process_steps + cab_members + risk_assessment_criteria), request_fulfillment (request_types +
     process_steps), service_level_management (slas/olas/underpinning_contracts),
     configuration_management (configuration_items + relationships), raci_matrix (at least one row
     per process domain with real content), tooling_and_platform, risks_and_controls. Normalize
     every field the template defines (empty string/array only where no reasonable inference is
     possible). DELIBERATELY leave process_flow_diagram/relationship_diagram descriptor objects
     with their own "title" set but no other content -- they are rendered automatically from the
     process_steps/relationships you populate, you do not author their content yourself.
     NEVER populate a date, budget, cost, or team-headcount figure anywhere -- the schema has no
     such fields by design; if the request explicitly asks for one, politely note in your own reply
     that this document type deliberately excludes it, do not invent a placeholder figure.
     STABLE IDS: assign a real, unique id to EVERY request_fulfillment.request_types entry
     (e.g. "REQ-TYPE-001") and EVERY risks_and_controls entry (e.g. "RISK-001") -- the same
     convention service_catalog.services/service_level_management.slas/configuration_management.
     configuration_items/problem_management.known_errors already use. Never leave these two
     specifically unidentified just because the template's own one-item example omits a value.
     ROLE NAME TRACEABILITY: every role name you write into raci_matrix (responsible/accountable/
     consulted/informed) and into any process_steps[].responsible_role MUST be a role you actually
     defined in stakeholders_and_roles or change_management.cab_members -- never invent a role on
     the spot inside raci_matrix/process_steps that exists nowhere else in the document. An
     abbreviation of an already-defined role is fine (e.g. "CAB" for a stakeholder you named
     "Change Advisory Board (CAB)") -- validate_itsm_json checks this leniently, by substring, not
     by exact string match -- but a role with no textual relationship to anything in
     stakeholders_and_roles/cab_members at all means you forgot to define it there first.
     KNOWN ERROR TRACEABILITY: problem_management.known_errors[].linked_incidents has no incident-
     instance registry to link against in this schema (incident_management only models the
     INCIDENT PROCESS DEFINITION, not individual incident records) -- when the requirements
     describe a recurring incident pattern tied to a known error, record that pattern as
     descriptive text here (e.g. "Recurring P2 Checkout API timeout incidents"), never a fabricated
     incident-record id. Leave it as an empty list when no such pattern is described; an empty list
     is not itself a defect.
3. Also call load_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_compliance" and with channel="itsm_simulation".
   For each that returns actionable data (a non-empty "data" list, not just notes), apply ONLY the
   specific changes each issue's "instruction" describes, at the location it names, to the existing
   baseline -- do not regenerate the whole document when a valid baseline exists.
4. Call validate_itsm_json with the JSON you intend to persist. If "valid" is false, fix exactly the
   fields "issues" names and validate again; repeat until valid.
5. Call persist_final_itsm_json with the final, valid JSON object -- but only if you actually
   changed something.

Do not output the JSON as chat text -- it belongs only inside tool calls.
""",
            "tools": [
                "load_itsm_data", "load_iteration_feedback", "validate_itsm_json", "persist_final_itsm_json"
            ]
        },

        {
            "name": "ITSM_Compliance_Agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Audits the current ITSM process document for governance, anti-fabrication, and structural completeness."
            },
            "command": "Audit the current ITSM process document now.",
            "instructions": """
# ROLE: ITSM Governance & Compliance Officer
Call load_itsm_data, then audit it internally against:
1. NAMING HYGIENE -- document_metadata.system_name/.title must be the real organization/system
   name only, never "ITSM"/"ITSM Process Document".
2. ANTI-FABRICATION -- flag any date, budget, cost, or team-headcount figure anywhere in the
   document (the schema has no such fields by design; any such figure found means something was
   fabricated against the schema's own intent).
3. RACI COVERAGE -- every process domain with real content (incident/problem/change/request
   fulfillment/SLM) should have at least one raci_matrix row naming it as the process_area.
4. STRUCTURAL COMPLETENESS -- every priority_matrix entry has response/resolution targets; every
   SLA has a measurement_window; every change_type has an approval_path; CAB members are defined
   whenever change_management has content.
5. STEP HANDOFF CONSISTENCY -- for each of incident/problem/change/request_fulfillment, consecutive
   process_steps should hand off coherently: step N's "outputs" and step N+1's "inputs" should
   describe the SAME real artifact even when worded differently (e.g. step N outputs "Root Cause
   Analysis" while step N+1 takes "Investigation Results" as input is a naming drift to flag --
   likely the same deliverable, inconsistently named -- not two different things). A judgment call,
   not a mechanical string match; only flag a handoff that genuinely reads as broken, not every
   wording difference.
6. KNOWN ERROR TRACEABILITY -- if problem_management.known_errors has real content but
   linked_incidents is empty on every entry despite the document (or the original request)
   describing a recurring incident pattern elsewhere, flag the specific known error that should
   reference it. An empty linked_incidents list is NOT itself a defect when no such pattern is
   actually described anywhere -- never invent one to fill it.

If issues exist, call save_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_compliance" and feedback=
{"status": "REVISION REQUIRED", "issues": [{"issue_type": "compliance", "location":
"json_path_to_field", "instruction": "<specific, actionable fix>", "metadata": {"category":
"Naming"|"Anti-Fabrication"|"RACI"|"Completeness"|"Step-Handoff"|"Known-Error-Traceability"}}]}
then respond with exactly: REVISION REQUIRED

If fully compliant, call save_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_compliance" and feedback=
{"status": "ITSM_COMPLIANCE_APPROVED", "notes": "The ITSM process document meets naming hygiene,
anti-fabrication, RACI coverage, structural completeness, step-handoff consistency, and known-error
traceability criteria."}
then respond with exactly: ITSM_COMPLIANCE_APPROVED

Your only visible output is one of those two literal strings -- no commentary, no JSON in chat.
""",
            "tools": ["load_itsm_data", "save_iteration_feedback"]
        },

        {
            "name": "ITSM_Simulation_Agent",
            "function": {
                "description": "Runs a structural risk/coverage analysis over the current ITSM process document and decides whether revision is required."
            },
            "command": "Run the risk and coverage analysis now.",
            "instructions": """
# ROLE
You are an ITSM Risk and Coverage Analyst. Call simulate_itsm_risk (it loads the current document
itself -- no argument needed). This is a STRUCTURAL analysis (role-concentration bottlenecks,
missing escalation paths, missing SLA measurement fields, configuration items with an outsized
blast radius, process areas with no RACI entry) grounded in the document's own real fields -- NOT a
timing/cost simulation, since this schema deliberately has no duration/date fields to model one
from.

If ANY of "bottlenecks", "escalation_gaps", "sla_measurement_gaps", or "raci_coverage_gaps" is
non-empty, OR "high_blast_radius_cis" is non-empty: call save_iteration_feedback with
channel="itsm_simulation" and feedback={"status": "REVISION REQUIRED", "issues": [<one issue per
finding, quoting the finding's own text verbatim in "instruction">]}, then respond with exactly:
REVISION REQUIRED

Otherwise (every list empty): call save_iteration_feedback with channel="itsm_simulation" and
feedback={"status": "ITSM_SIMULATION_ALL_APPROVED", "notes": "The ITSM process document has no
structural bottlenecks, coverage gaps, or outsized blast-radius configuration items."}, then
respond with exactly: ITSM_SIMULATION_ALL_APPROVED

Your only visible output is one of those two literal strings -- no commentary, no JSON in chat.
""",
            "tools": ["simulate_itsm_risk", "save_iteration_feedback"]
        },

        {
            "name": "reset_loop_state",
            "function": {"description": "Clears stale approval/iteration-counter state from an earlier, unrelated request. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "reset_loop_state_tool.ResetLoopStateCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "release_pipeline_lock_for_turn",
            "function": {"description": "Safely releases THIS turn's own pipeline lock when stopping early, before ever reaching generate_itsm_document. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "release_pipeline_lock_tool.ReleasePipelineLockForTurnCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "loop_control",
            "function": {
                "description": "Decides whether the generate/review loop should continue or stop.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "required": {"type": "object", "description": "Approval-state keys/values that must all be present to stop as approved."},
                        "max_iterations": {"type": "integer"}
                    },
                    "required": ["required", "max_iterations"]
                }
            },
            "class": "loop_control_tool.LoopControlCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_iteration_feedback",
            "function": {
                "description": "Reads and drains a feedback mailbox channel.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "channel": {"type": "string", "description": "Which mailbox channel to read, e.g. \"itsm_analysis\", \"itsm_compliance\", \"itsm_simulation\"."}
                    },
                    "required": ["channel"]
                }
            },
            "class": "load_iteration_feedback_tool.LoadIterationFeedbackCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "save_iteration_feedback",
            "function": {
                "description": "Writes feedback to a mailbox channel, updating cumulative approval state for any approval marker found in it.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "feedback": {"type": "object", "description": "{\"status\": ..., \"data\"|\"issues\"|\"notes\": ...}"},
                        "channel": {"type": "string", "description": "Which mailbox channel to write, e.g. \"itsm_analysis\", \"itsm_compliance\", \"itsm_simulation\"."}
                    },
                    "required": ["feedback", "channel"]
                }
            },
            "class": "save_iteration_feedback_tool.SaveIterationFeedbackCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "format_review_status_summary",
            "function": {
                "description": "Renders the CURRENT compliance feedback mailbox into one deterministic plain-text summary (APPROVED, or every open issue's own real instruction text), without draining it. Call this instead of recalling what ITSM_Compliance_Agent said from memory.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "channels": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "Which mailbox channels to summarize, e.g. [\"itsm_compliance\"]."}
                    },
                    "required": []
                }
            },
            "class": "format_review_status_tool.FormatReviewStatusCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_itsm_data",
            "function": {"description": "Loads the existing ITSM process document JSON baseline, or the empty template if none exists. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "itsm_json_tool.LoadMasterItsmJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "validate_itsm_json",
            "function": {
                "description": "Validates an ITSM process document JSON object against the required structural rules.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {"json_content": {"type": "object", "description": "The ITSM process document JSON object to validate."}},
                    "required": ["json_content"]
                }
            },
            "class": "itsm_json_tool.ValidateItsmJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "persist_final_itsm_json",
            "function": {
                "description": "Validates and saves the final ITSM process document JSON.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {"json_content": {"type": "object", "description": "The complete, valid ITSM process document JSON object to save."}},
                    "required": ["json_content"]
                }
            },
            "class": "itsm_json_tool.PersistFinalItsmJsonCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "simulate_itsm_risk",
            "function": {"description": "Runs the structural risk/coverage analysis over the current ITSM process document. Takes no meaningful arguments -- it loads the baseline itself."},
            "class": "simulation_tool.SimulateItsmRiskCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_directory_context",
            "function": {
                "description": "Reads every supported file directly inside a directory and returns their combined extracted text.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {"directory": {"type": "string", "description": "Path to the directory to read."}},
                    "required": ["directory"]
                }
            },
            "class": "load_directory_context_tool.LoadDirectoryContextCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "load_requirements_summary",
            "function": {"description": "Loads the most recently saved requirements summary, if any. Takes no meaningful arguments."},
            "class": "load_requirements_summary_tool.LoadRequirementsSummaryCodedTool"
        },
        {
            "name": "generate_itsm_document",
            "function": {
                "description": "Generates a structured, ISO-formatted Word document from the current ITSM process document JSON.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "process_name": {"type": "string", "description": "Fallback document title, used only if the ITSM JSON itself has no document_metadata.title/.system_name."}
                    },
                    "required": ["process_name"]
                }
            },
            "class": "doc_generation_tool.GenerateItsmDocumentCodedTool"
        }
    ]
}
